🧵 Part 1: Strings (15 Questions)
String manipulation is arguably the most common topic in QA automation interviews due to text assertion and log parsing requirements.
	1	Reverse a String: Reverse a string in place without using Python’s built-in [::-1].
	2	Valid Palindrome: Check if a string is a palindrome, ignoring casing and non-alphanumeric characters.
	3	First Unique Character: Find the index of the first non-repeating character in a string.
	4	Anagram Check: Determine if two strings are anagrams of each other (e.g., "silent" and "listen").
	5	String Compression: Implement a method to perform basic string compression using the counts of repeated characters (e.g., aabcccccaaa $\rightarrow$ a2b1c5a3).
	6	Longest Common Prefix: Find the longest common prefix string amongst an array of strings.
	7	Reverse Words in a String: Given an input string, reverse the order of the words (e.g., "the sky is blue" $\rightarrow$ "blue is sky the").
	8	Count Vowels and Consonants: Count the number of vowels and consonants in a given text block.
	9	Valid Parentheses: Given a string containing just the characters (, ), {, }, [ and ], determine if the input string is valid (Stack/String problem).
	10	Length of Last Word: Given a string consisting of words and spaces, return the length of the last word.
	11	Substrings Generation: Print all possible non-empty substrings of a given string.
	12	Remove Duplicate Characters: Remove duplicate characters from a string while keeping the original order.
	13	String to Integer (atoi): Convert a string to a 32-bit signed integer (handling whitespace, signs, and overflow).
	14	Find All Anagrams in a String: Find all the start indices of an anagram of a pattern in a larger string.
	15	Isomorphic Strings: Determine if two strings are isomorphic (characters in string A can be replaced to get string B).
📊 Part 2: Arrays (15 Questions)
Array manipulation questions test your ability to use pointers, manage indices, and clean up datasets.
	16	Two Sum: Find two numbers in an array that add up to a specific target number.
	17	Move Zeroes: Move all 0s to the end of an array while maintaining the relative order of the non-zero elements.
	18	Find the Duplicate Number: Find the duplicate number in an array containing $n + 1$ integers where each integer is between $1$ and $n$.
	19	Missing Number: Find the missing number in an array containing $n$ distinct numbers taken from $0, 1, 2, \dots, n$.
	20	Maximum Subarray (Kadane’s Algorithm): Find the contiguous subarray which has the largest sum.
	21	Merge Sorted Arrays: Merge two sorted arrays into one large sorted array without using extra space.
	22	Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array: Remove duplicates in-place such that each unique element appears only once.
	23	Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock: Find the maximum profit you can achieve from buying and selling a stock on different days.
	24	Container With Most Water: Find two lines that together with the x-axis form a container that holds the most water.
	25	Rotate Array: Rotate an array to the right by $k$ steps.
	26	Majority Element: Find the element that appears more than $\lfloor n / 2 \rfloor$ times in an array.
	27	Intersection of Two Arrays: Given two arrays, return an array of their intersection (each element must be unique).
	28	Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array: Find the minimum element in an array that has been sorted and then rotated.
	29	Product of Array Except Self: Return an array where each element is equal to the product of all elements except the one at that index (without using division).
	30	Sort an Array of 0s, 1s, and 2s (Dutch National Flag): Sort an array containing only three distinct values in linear time.
🔗 Part 3: Lists / Linked Lists (10 Questions)
Linked lists are great for testing pointer manipulation, nodes tracking, and understanding memory references.
	31	Reverse a Linked List: Reverse a singly linked list iteratively and recursively.
	32	Detect Cycle in a Linked List: Determine if a linked list has a cycle in it (Floyd's Tortoise and Hare algorithm).
	33	Merge Two Sorted Lists: Merge two sorted linked lists into a single sorted list.
	34	Remove N-th Node From End of List: Remove the $n$-th node from the end of a singly linked list in a single pass.
	35	Middle of the Linked List: Return the middle node of a linked list.
	36	Palindrome Linked List: Check if a singly linked list forms a palindrome.
	37	Intersection of Two Linked Lists: Find the node at which two singly linked lists intersect.
	38	Delete Node in a Linked List: Delete a node in a singly linked list given access only to that node.
	39	Remove Duplicates from Sorted List: Delete all duplicate nodes from a sorted linked list so elements appear once.
	40	Flatten a Multilevel Doubly Linked List: Flatten a linked list where nodes can have a child pointer pointing to a separate list.
🗺️ Part 4: Maps / Dictionaries (10 Questions)
Hashing data structures are crucial for QA automation because they unlock $O(1)$ lookups, ideal for comparing massive test UI baselines against database records.
	41	Group Anagrams: Group an array of strings together if they are anagrams of each other.
	42	Contains Duplicate II: Determine if there are two distinct indices $i$ and $j$ in the array such that nums[i] == nums[j] and the absolute difference between $i$ and $j$ is at most $k$.
	43	Frequency of Characters/Elements: Count the frequency of every element in an array or string and sort by highest frequency.
	44	Subarray Sum Equals K: Find the total number of continuous subarrays whose sum equals a given target $k$ (using prefix sums and a map).
	45	Top K Frequent Elements: Given an integer array, return the $k$ most frequent elements.
	46	Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters: Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters (sliding window + map).
	47	Find All Duplicates in an Array: Use a hash map/set to track and return all elements that appear twice in an array.
	48	Valid Sudoku: Determine if a 9x9 Sudoku board is valid based on rows, columns, and 3x3 sub-boxes.
	49	Word Pattern: Given a pattern and a string s, find if s follows the exact same pattern (e.g., pattern abba matches "dog cat cat dog").
	50	Design a Simple LRU Cache: Implement a Least Recently Used (LRU) cache using a Hash Map combined with a Doubly Linked List.
